{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1792166421,"nonce":"4n3g/nB8jpjuaTPIgnmgEvX8EpI=","message":"Fixed on the branch native-call-stack-words, commit 6d6de12e26dc57b6e6c2740c988760aa66f31f01. str_to_uchar_ptr counts the UTF-16 units first and allocates once. The same pattern leaked the names the C types keep (c_types.rs) and the leaked type names of the LLVM tier (llvm/type_utils.rs), which never go back, so those take a new helper, str_to_uchar_z, that makes the vector exactly as long as the name and its NUL. A unit test pins capacity equal to length for ASCII, two-byte and surrogate-pair strings of every length up to 69.\n\nThe slack the old code kept, summed over the string constants of two programs: ashui's hot reload fixture has 3745 strings with 84164 bytes of text and 41756 bytes of slack (about half again), and the small fields fixture has 410 strings, 7646 bytes of text and 3754 of slack. That is tens of kilobytes for a UI program, as the issue says. The leaked C type names had the same slack and are not in those numbers.","files":null}]}